      "rendered_prefix": "<\uff5cbegin\u2581of\u2581sentence\uff5c><\uff5cUser\uff5c>Write a Python 3 implementation of ordered_groups for a list of (key, value) rows. Each key is a string, including possibly the empty string; each value is a signed 32-bit integer. Inputs already have valid types. Return a list of groups. Each group contains a key and the list of all values seen for that key. The groups must appear in the order in which their keys first appeared. Values within a group keep input order, including repeated and negative values. Compare keys exactly: do not case-fold, normalize Unicode, or sort. Do not modify the input. Empty input produces an empty list.\nFor [(\"north\", 4), (\"\", 9), (\"north\", -2), (\"east\", 5), (\"north\", 4)], the result must be [(\"north\", [4,-2,4]), (\"\", [9]), (\"east\", [5])]. Use only the standard library and explain how the implementation preserves both kinds of order. In particular, do not replace a group's value list with a sum or a set. Avoid scanning all earlier groups for every input row.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c></think>",
